<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian unification</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Risorgimento</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Italian unification, or the Risorgimento, was the political process that united the states of the Italian peninsula into a single kingdom, proclaimed in 1861. Leading figures included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>. The new kingdom's ambitions for great-power status led to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-unification.jpg" alt="Italian unification" width="294" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baldassare Verazzi (1819-1886) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Episodio_delle_cinque_giornate_(Baldassare_Verazzi).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Parma and Piacenza, created by Pope Paul III for his son in 1545, gave the Farnese and then the Bourbon-Parma dynasties a lasting Italian state, produced the general Alessandro Farnese and notable art patronage and printing, and survived as a pawn of greater powers until <b>Italian unification</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1860, by the Treaty of Turin, Victor Emmanuel II and Count Cavour ceded Savoy and Nice to France in return for French support for <b>Italian unification</b>, and the ancestral duchy became the French departments of Savoie and Haute-Savoie.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Italian Empire emerged from the ambitions of the Kingdom of Italy, newly unified in the <b>Risorgimento</b> and proclaimed in 1861 and completed with the capture of Rome in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Italy grew out of the <b>Risorgimento</b>, the movement to unite a peninsula divided among Austrian provinces, the Papal States, the Bourbon Kingdom of the Two Sicilies and several duchies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In its Savoyard phase the kingdom became the instrument of <b>Italian unification</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At the encounter of Teano Garibaldi met King Victor Emmanuel and handed him the conquered kingdom; the people voted in a plebiscite to join the Kingdom of Sardinia, and the annexation completed the first phase of <b>Italian unification</b>, the Kingdom of Italy being proclaimed in 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
